    "title": "Tricycle Operation: Duly passed and approved Resolution No. 121-2018 provides",
    "fullText": "for the following provisions for tricycle operations within the Municipality of Dalaguete. \n\nDefinition of Terms: \n\nA. Motorized Tricycle - is a motor vehicle propelled other than by muscular power, composed of \na motorcycle fitted with a single wheel sidecar or a motorcycle with a two wheeled cab, the \nformer having a total of four wheels, otherwise known as the motorela. \nB. Tricycle Operators - are persons engaged in the business of operating tricycles. \n\n2022 Revised Revenue Code \n40 \nC. Tricycle-for-Hire - is a vehicle composed of a motorcycle fitted with a single-wheel side car or \na motorcycle with a two-wheel cab operated to render transport services to the general public \nfor a fee. \nD. Motorized Tricycle Operator's Permit (MTOP) - is a document granting franchise or license \nto a person, natural or juridical, allowing him to operate tricycles-for-hire over specified zones. \nE. Zone - is a contiguous land area or block, such as subdivision or barangay, where tricycles-for-\nhire may operate without a fixed origin and destination. \nF. Board – refers to the Dalaguete Franchising and Regulatory Board. \nG. Commission – refers to the Dalaguete Franchising and Regulatory Commission, which is \nthe executing arm of the Board. \nH. Certificate of Public Convenience – means a document granted by the DFRB to person/s \nto operate a tricycle-for-hire within the jurisdiction of the Municipality, which constitutes \nhis/its franchise good for a period of three (3) years from date of issuance. \nI. Driver – shall mean any person who drives a motorized tricycle and is duly licensed by the \nLand Transportation Office. Any person driving a tricycle-for-hire must be a professional \ndriver or holder of a Philippine Professional Driver’s License. \nJ. Fee – a charge fixed by law or ordinance for the regulation or inspection of a business or \nactivity. \nK. Franchise – a privilege affected with public interest, which is conferred upon private \npersons or corporations, under such terms and conditions as the government, and its \npolitical subdivisions may impose in the interest of public welfare, security and safety. \nL. Levy – means an imposition or collection of an assessment, tax, tribute or fine. \nM. Owner/Operator – shall mean the actual legal owner of a motor vehicle, in his name such \nmotor vehicle is duly registered with the Land Transportation Office. For purposes of \nengaging in the transport of passengers or cargoes or for business and/or private use, must \nbe a holder of a Certificate of Public Convenience. \nN. Person – means every natural person as provided for under the provisions of the Civil Code \nof the Philippines, however for purposes of transport business, undertaking shall also \ninclude juridical persons. \nO. Tax – Means an enforced contribution, usually monetary in form, levied by the lawmaking \nbody on person and property under its jurisdiction for the precise purpose of supporting \ngovernmental needs. \nP. Temporary Authority to Operate – a permit granted by the Board to an operator without a \nlegitimate franchise for a short period as may be determined by the Board from its date of \nissuance. \nQ. Tricycle-for-Private Use – a motor vehicle composed of motorcycle fitted with a single-\nwheel sidecar or a motorcycle with a two-wheel cab operated for personal or for private use. \nR. Tricycle-for-Business Use – a motor vehicle composed of motorcycle fitted with a single-\nwheel sidecar or a motorcycle with a two-wheel cab operated for business or to transport \ngoods and/or articles of commerce for their own business establishments. \nS. Taxi Service Permit – a special permit to operate issued to operator of a Tricycle-for-hire, \nfor operations like taxi service.",
